\f1\fs22 Hayley attended Halo\'92s address, and was surprised to find a decently presentable concierge staffing the greeting station in the lobby.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91May I help you?\'92 the concierge asked in a way that sounds like she actually wanted to be helpful.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92d kill for a decent cup of coffee,\'92 Hayley quipped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Without missing a beat, the concierge said, \'91Oh, there\'92s an excellent cafe above the Rail station. They do excellent blue cheese and Vegemite croissants.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley hadn\'92t tasted one of those since her grandmother died in corner store supplier invoice dispute. Those taxers were always upping the prices if you stopped complaining. Unfortunately, Hayley\'92s grandmother complained too loud,\'97over the supply of Vegemite and baked goods, as it turned out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley smiled like a sheep and waved the conversation away. \'91No, sorry. I\'92m here to visit someone \'85 Haloren Smythe, apartment 25 \'85 oh \'85 64?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The concierge instantly tensed. Hayley never misses something like that. \'91Twenty-five sixty-four?\'92 the concierge said like a kookaburra with a snake stuck in its gullet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Uh-huh.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91One moment, I\'92ll just call to let him know you\'92re here. May I have your name please?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley pulled out her tablet and flashed her badge screen. \'91Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, you\'92re 
\f2\i police
\f1\i0 ,\'92 the concierge said with undisguised relief as she tapped on a keypad on a tablet hidden behind the station\'97many more than four numbers, Hayley noted. \'91Good morning, Mr Smythe? It\'92s Jody from downstairs at Faircloud Apartments. There\'92s someone here calling on you, a Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning? \'85 Yes \'85 Very well. Yes, I\'92ll tell her.\'92 The concierge killed the call and said, \'91Mr Smythe is out of town today. He wonders if you could call his office to arrange an appointment.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Did you already know he was out of town?\'92 Hayley asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Wha\'85 no?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You must\'92ve, because you called an external line.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, 
\f2\i that
\f1\i0 . No. It\'92s an automatic redirect if the apartment intercom isn\'92t answered in time.\'92 Jody\'92s bottom lip trembled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But you tapped more than four numbers\'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I had to open the comms screen first before I entered the number.\'92 Jody tried to stop her chest from heaving.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley grimaced. \'91The pattern of the tapping would be different. I heard a contiguous strings of taps, without pause.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92No. I swear. The intercom system redirects.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Show me, then. Dial 2564.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody had the eyes of a gazelle with a cheetah chasing it down. Mechanically, she did as Hayley asked, as Hayley came around to the business side of the station and said, \'91Put it on speaker.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody hesitated so Hayley leaned across and pressed the right button on the tablet interface. Jody didn\'92t stop her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The line connected but no one answered. Hayley nodded her insistence that Jody speak. \'91Ah, Mr Smythe?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes,\'92 a female voice answered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s Jody from downstairs at Faircloud Apartments, again.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was a pause before the female voice said, \'91Is there a problem?\'94 in a tone that conveyed both that problems were serious yet all problems could be solved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody hoped this one could be. \'91Ah, your visitor is really quite \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Amenable,\'92 Hayley whispered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Amenable,\'92 Jody repeated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91To meeting you at the earliest convenience,\'92 Hayley whispered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody repeated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was a another pause. \'91We\'92ll send a team. ETA five minutes. Stall her.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The line went emphatically dead.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jody wailed and grabbed her bag from the station desk, fumbled at throwing its strap over her shoulder. She looked so panicked, she might fall out of her shoes. \'91No job is worth this taxing shit,\'92 she cried and awkwardly ran for the door, leaving Hayley alone at the station with an unlocked tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley had maybe five minutes but, since she was already at the show, she might as well use them. And use them she did. In less than twenty seconds, she\'92d rummaged the station cupboards and drawers, found the emergency maintenance keycard that would let her through any door in the building. Also in less than twenty seconds, a PAPER Tactical Response team mounted up and took an armoured hover-copt to the air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley walked, not ran, and summoned a lift. It must have been a slow day because three arrived at once. Inside forty-five seconds, she rode a lift, found the door to 2564 and gained access to inside. She could allow herself maybe ninety seconds to case the place.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Through the window, she didn\'92t notice a black speck flying directly at her at full hover-copt speed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley probably had all she needed to know: the place was decorated in unliveable creams and insipid beiges. It was empty\'97no lounges, floor coverings, entertainment screens, tables, chairs, or light shades. No evidence anyone lived there. It still had new paint smell, and new floor sealant smell. She could leave with just that, but she was always thorough\'97her grandmother had taught her that, although, to be fair, her grandmother\'92s thoroughness was probably what killed her\'97and she\'92d hate to miss something now she was here. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley didn\'92t notice the hover-copt coming at her at full hover-copt speed had already detected her infra-red presence in the apartment. The Tactical Response team sought and gained approval to arm weapons.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The fridges were empty, both the stasis unit and the chiller fridge. The kitchen cupboards and drawers were empty, the bedrooms accommodated no beds, the wardrobes arrayed no clothes nor stacked boxes of junk.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The hover-copt approached with deadly intent, closing with in a striking range that still offered minimal collateral damage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bathroom offered no soaps, no shampoos, no combs with hair tangled in their teeth, no toothbrushes, not even a roll of toilet paper. The tiling in the shower recess had yet to be grouted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the hover-copt closed within range, the Hayley-shaped infrared blob moved from the main bedroom, down the corridor and to the living room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fire!\'92 the team leader ordered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The hover-copt\'92s particle cannons neatly strafed the apartment, shattered all the windows, and punctured the walls with evenly spaced chest-high shots. In the kitchen, the chiller fridge wheezed as its door screens exploded, while the stasis fridge did what it does best: it stayed inert beyond the conventional laws of physics.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The front door splintered and broke like kindling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s body lay face-first on the hallway carpet, thrown there by the terrifying blast.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was over. The hover-copt flew around the building and ended in the forecourt. The Tactical Response team alighted and proceeded with impunity to secure the building.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s body scurried to the elevator lobby, found enough bravery to to press a console button to call a lift, leapt in as the doors opened and rode the elevator, cowering on the floor in the corner with its arms over its head, all the way to the basement. Like a tiny nocturnal marsupial, Hayley\'92s body flittered out of the elevator, straight for the garbage room, gained access to it using the maintenance keycard, and escaped, by the service dock, into the steaming mid-Autumn heat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After Hayley\'92s cognitive state finally reunited with Hayley\'92s body, she would thereafter experience the scent of garbage as less gag-worthy than new apartment smell.
